== Frequency-dependent specific detectivity
<frequency-dependent-specific-detectivity>
The specific detectivity $\( D^(*) \)$ @nudelman_detectivity_1962 is a key figure of merit that normalizes the bolometer's sensitivity to its physical area, allowing for direct comparison between different detector technologies. We calculate it by combining the results from the preceding sections using the relation: $ D^(*) = frac(sqrt(A_d) \| cal(R)_lambda \( f \) \|, sqrt(S_lambda \( f \))) $ where $A_d approx 4 times 10^(- 6) med upright(c m)^2$ is the physical area of the pixel, $\| cal(R)_lambda \( f \) \|$ is the frequency-dependent responsivity, and $sqrt(S_lambda \( f \))$ is the wavelength noise amplitude spectral density.

The resulting $D^(*)$ spectrum, plotted in @fig:Detectivity, reveals a peak performance of $D^(*) approx 1.1 times 10^7 med upright(J o n e s)$ (where $1 med upright("Jones") = 1 med upright("cm") dot.op upright("Hz")^(1 \/ 2) dot.op upright("W")^(- 1)$) around $5 med upright(k H z)$, near the device's thermal cutoff frequency. The shape of the spectrum is governed by the previously characterized device parameters. At low frequencies, $D^(*)$ is suppressed by the technical noise of the readout system, while at frequencies above the peak, it rolls off as a direct consequence of the thermal time constant limiting the responsivity.


= Discussions
<sec:discussion>

Our photonic crystal bolometer achieves a specific detectivity of $D^(*) approx 1.1 times 10^7 med upright(J o n e s)$ and a thermal time constant of $34 med mu upright(s)$ at ambient temperature and pressure. This performance point, shown in @fig:benchmark, is distinguished by its speed, as the $4.7 med upright(k H z)$ bandwidth is more than an order of magnitude faster than that of many high-sensitivity uncooled bolometers, including resistive and nano-thermoelectric platforms.

For a single-mode bolometer limited by TRN @PhysRevX.10.041046, the fundamental detectivity is set by the thermal conductance, $D_(upright(l i m i t))^(*) prop 1 \/ sqrt(G_(upright(t h)))$, or equivalently $D_(upright(l i m i t))^(*) prop sqrt(tau_(upright(t h)) \/ C_(upright(t h)))$ at fixed $C_(upright(t h))$. Thus, for a given pixel heat capacity, the TRN-limited performance follows a single $D_(upright(l i m i t))^(*) \( tau_(upright(t h)) \)$ curve along which changes in $G_(upright(t h))$ move the operating point. For the present pixel, based on the measured thermal resistance and calculated heat capacity, this trade-off is shown by a dashed green line in @fig:benchmark, and the green cross marks the corresponding TRN-limited operating point $D_(upright(l i m i t))^(*) approx 3.1 times 10^8 med upright(J o n e s)$. Lowering $G_(upright(t h))$ -- for example by operating in vacuum to suppress convection -- would increase $D_(upright(l i m i t))^(*)$ at the expense of a longer thermal time constant, whereas increasing $G_(upright(t h))$ (e.g. at higher gas pressure) would trade sensitivity for speed. Because our architecture enables a small pixel heat capacity, the corresponding TRN curve in @fig:benchmark lies above the envelope of reported uncooled LWIR detectors over the $tau_(upright(t h))$ range shown, so that at a given thermal time constant a TRN-limited implementation of this architecture could, in principle, achieve higher specific detectivity.

